{"parentUuid":"509cdb4f-fe5d-4b19-8a66-06adbf2aaab9","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"f859c755-da6c-49d3-9404-148b849e4500","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","content":"BLOCKED by dcg\n\nTip: dcg explain \"find /Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/f1097c6d/tmp/vid2 -type f \\( -name '*.mp4' -o -name '*.webm' -o -name '*.mkv' -o -name '*.m4a' \\) -delete\ndu -sh /Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/f1097c6d/tmp/vid2\necho \\\"--- kept ---\\\"; ls /Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/f1097c6d/tmp/vid2/frames | head\"\n\nReason: find ... -delete is destructive (bytewise-equivalent to rm -rf on the matched tree) and requires human approval.\n\nExplanation: `find ... -delete` recursively deletes every path matched by the find expression. The action flag `-delete` implies `-depth` (so directories are deleted after their contents). With no path predicate it deletes the entire starting tree. Common pitfalls:\n             \n             - `find . -delete` deletes the current working directory's contents.\n             - `find <path> -delete` with a wide -name glob matches more than expected.\n             - `-delete` errors are silent by default — failures don't stop the walk.\n             \n             Safer alternatives:\n             - Drop -delete to preview: `find <path> ...` (just lists matches)\n             - Add -print -delete to log each deletion as it happens\n             - Use `find /tmp/<subdir> ... -delete` (allowed under temp dirs)\n             - For a few files: `find ... | xargs -t -p rm -i` for confirmation\n\nRule: core.filesystem:find-delete-general\n\nIf this operation is truly needed, ask the user for explicit permission and have them run the command manually.","is_error":true,"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Nd1xtJqGKfSHG9r5MTakhv"}]},"uuid":"c1359228-d2e6-473d-a425-26661aa3628f","timestamp":"2026-09-12T13:38:06.801Z","toolUseResult":"Error: BLOCKED by dcg\n\nTip: dcg explain \"find /Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/f1097c6d/tmp/vid2