{"parentUuid":"0e4b168b-a6fc-4217-8e35-5e09363723d9","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"e343e296-cde7-42eb-aa45-e11d4469cccf","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ujat4ZeYtBhbQPd5ZpKftY","type":"tool_result","content":"fixed: SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-harness-over-model-mark-kashef.md\nfixed: SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-skill-audit.md\nfixed: .claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/memory/project_skill-bloat-and-promotion.md\nfixed: .claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/memory/MEMORY.md\nfixed: .claude/global-memory/MEMORY.md\n--- cap check ---\n    2488\nShell cwd was reset to /Users/kristinarash/DEV","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"38cdda65-3a4d-409e-9104-0cd10d9b472e","timestamp":"2026-09-12T14:05:26.054Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"fixed: SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-harness-over-model-mark-kashef.md\nfixed: SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-skill-audit.md\nfixed: .claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/memory/project_skill-bloat-and-promotion.md\nfixed: .claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/memory/MEMORY.md\nfixed: .claude/global-memory/MEMORY.md\n--- cap check ---\n    2488","stderr":"\nShell cwd was reset to /Users/kristinarash/DEV","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0e4b168b-a6fc-4217-8e35-5e09363723d9","session_id":"f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"38cdda65-3a4d-409e-9104-0cd10d9b472e","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-skill-audit.md","snippet":"1\t---\n2\ttitle: \"Skill audit 2026-09-12 — 160 global skills down to 60\"\n3\tdate: 2026-09-12\n4\ttype: audit\n5\ttrigger: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6LNlCpQPYFc\n6\trelated: 2026-09-12-harness-over-model-mark-kashef.md\n7\tscript: ~/.claude/skills-audit-2026-09-12.sh\n8\ttags: [skills, context-engineering, claude-code, audit]\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t# Skill audit — 2026-09-12\n12\t\n13\tTriggered by the Mark Kashef / Nate Herk harness video. Companion note:\n14\t`2026-09-12-harness-over-model-mark-kashef.md`.\n15\t\n16\t## The bill\n17\t\n18\t| | |\n19\t|---|---|\n20\t| Global skills in `~/.claude/skills/` | **160** |\n21\t| — of which are symlinks into `~/.agents/skills/` | 45 |\n22\t| Plugin skills exposed per session | ~37 (6 plugins enabled, 18 disabled) |\n23\t| SKILL.md files in plugin cache | 606 — mostly disabled plugins and old versions, **not** the load |\n24\t| Project-level skills anywhere in `~/DEV` | **0** |\n25\t| Distinct skills ever invoked (414 sessions) | **58** |\n26\t| Used 3+ times | 30 |\n27\t| Never used at all | **130** |\n28\t| **Context cost of the skill roster, every session** | **~19,158 tokens** |\n29\t\n30\tThat last row is the real cost. Every skill's name and description is loaded into\n31\tcontext at session start whether you use it or not. 19k tokens is the standing tax.\n32\t\n33\t## The rule applied\n34\t\n35\tThe video's audit question is not *\"how often did I run this?\"* It is:\n36\t\n37\t> **\"Would the model do this fine without the skill now?\"**\n38\t\n39\tA skill is a crutch. As the brain gets smarter, the crutch starts to limit it.\n40\tSo the keep/cut split below is by **category**, not by usage count.\n41\t\n42\t## Verified before cutting\n43\t\n44\t- `interceptor`, `firecrawl`, `playwright`, `bright-data`, `apify` — **all missing\n45\t  from PATH.** Those skills can never fire successfully. Pure dead weight.\n46\t- **45 skills are symlinks** into `~/.agents/skills/`, the folder Codex and Cowork\n47\t  also read. Archiving a symlink de-registers it from Claude Code and **leaves the\n48\t  real skill intact for Codex.** That is the desired behaviour, not a side effect.\n49\t  (Related: `project_vendored-skill-install`, `project_vendored-skill-repair`.)\n50\t- 13 skills were installed in the last 14 days. They get a grace period — too new\n51\t  to judge. Not in any cut tier.\n52\t\n53\t## The tiers\n54\t\n55\t### Tier 1 — dead weight (43 skills, ~6,663 tokens)\n56\t\n57\tHighest confidence. No judgment calls.\n58\t\n59\t- **Broken tools (9):** `Interceptor` `Browser` `playwright-cli` `firecrawl`\n60\t  `firecrawl-build-onboarding` `BrightData` `Apify` `web-scraping` `everything-search`\n61\t  — binaries not installed.\n62\t- **Generic thinking / prompt skills (21):** `caveman` `zoom-out` `roast` `BeCreative`\n63\t  `Ideate` `FirstPrinciples` `SystemsThinking` `Aphorisms` `Prompting` `BiasCheck`\n64\t  `IterativeDepth` `ApertureOscillation` `BitterPillEngineering` `ExtractWisdom`\n65\t  `Council` `grill-me` `grill-with-docs` `backtrack` `fact-checker` `Fabric`\n66\t  — this is *exactly* the category the video says to delete. Opus 5 does these natively.\n67\t- **Not your domain (13):** `pp-flight-goat` `pp-movie-goat` `pp-recipe-goat` `Sales`\n68\t  `starter-icp-positioning` `starter-voice-profile` `USMetrics` `WorldThreatModel`\n69\t  `RedTeam` `PrivateInvestigator` `Art` `AudioEditor` `Remotion` `WriteStory`\n70\t\n71\t### Tier 2 — superseded (57 skills, ~6,137 tokens)\n72\t\n73\tYou already use something that covers each of these. Contains judgment calls —\n74\treview before running.\n75\t\n76\t- **Skill authoring** — `CreateSkill` (22 uses) covers: `write-a-skill`\n77\t  `writing-great-skills` `skill-evaluator` `find-skills`\n78\t- **Matt Pocock duplicates** — you invoke the `mattpocock-skills:*` plugin versions;\n79\t  these are stale local copies: `ask-matt` `setup-matt-pocock-skills` `codebase-design`\n80\t  `diagnosing-bugs` `domain-modeling` `tdd` `research`\n81\t- **Design pile** — `impeccable` (6) + `artifact-design` (8) cover: `gpt-taste`\n82\t  `high-end-visual-design` `image-to-code` `imagegen-frontend-*` `industrial-brutalist-ui`\n83\t  `minimalist-ui` `redesign-existing-projects` `stitch-design-taste` `design-academy-skill`\n84\t  `design-taste-frontend-v1` `starter-design-tokens` `brandkit` `Webdesign`\n85\t  `frontend-slides` `visual-explainer` `diagram-generator`\n86\t- **Workflow pile** — `superpowers:*` + `claudex-*` cover: `to-prd` `to-spec` `to-tickets`\n87\t  `triage` `implement` `wayfinder` `improve-codebase-architecture` `RootCauseAnalysis`\n88\t  `loop-designer` `Loop` `Delegation` `Migrate` `Optimize` `Hardening` `decision-toolkit`\n89\t  `CreateCLI` `Agents` `Evals` `ContextSearch` `Daemon` `LocalIntelligence` `Knowledge`\n90\t  `Interview` `process-interviewer` `research-assistant` `storm-research`\n91\t  `full-output-enforcement` `fable-mode` `Telos`\n92\t\n93\t### Keep — tacit knowledge, even at zero uses\n94\t\n95\tThese carry knowledge the model can never have. The video is explicit that this is\n96\tthe one category worth keeping forever.\n97\t\n98\t`game-film-timestamps` (Hudl/DVSport/ESPN timestamp repair) ·\n99\t`pp-catapult-openfield-connect` (GPS) · `pp-zotero-research-library` ·\n100\t`five-slides-generator` (your Baylor deck format) · `pp-fireflies`\n101\t\n102\t### Grace period — installed in the last 14 days\n103\t\n104\t`new-project` `brain-audit` `codex-build` `claudex-route` `claudex-loop`\n105\t`next-decision` `decisions` `before-building` `anti-sleep` `persistent-localhost`\n106\t`session-miner` `improve-system` `design-taste-frontend-v1`\n107\t\n108\t### Your call — not cut, not defended\n109\t\n110\t`pbir-report-builder` (Power BI / IBCS — plausibly useful for Baylor reporting,\n111\tunused 47 days) · `Science` · `ArXiv` · `pp-espn` · `weekly-review` ·\n112\t`ingest-resource` · `library-advisor` · `library-review` · `capture-insight` ·\n113\t`scroll-craft`\n114\t\n115\t## Result\n116\t\n117\t| Stage | Skills | Roster cost | Per-session tokens |\n118\t|---|---|---|---|\n119\t| Now | 160 | 76,632 ch | ~19,158 |\n120\t| After Tier 1 | 117 | 49,980 ch | ~12,495 |\n121\t| After Tier 2 | **60** | 25,430 ch | **~6,357** |\n122\t\n123\t**~12,800 tokens back every session — a 66% cut.**\n124\t\n125\t## How to run it\n126\t\n127\tThe script **archives, never deletes.** Everything moves to\n128\t`~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12/`.\n129\t\n130\t```bash\n131\tbash ~/.claude/skills-audit-2026-09-12.sh dryrun   # show, move nothing\n132\tbash ~/.claude/skills-audit-2026-09-12.sh tier1\n133\tbash ~/.claude/skills-audit-2026-09-12.sh tier2\n134\t```\n135\t\n136\tUndo, any time:\n137\t\n138\t```bash\n139\tmv ~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12/<name> ~/.claude/skills/   # one skill\n140\tmv ~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12/* ~/.claude/skills/        # all of them\n141\t```\n142\t\n143\tThen `/reload-skills` or start a new session.\n144\t\n145\t## Next steps after this\n146\t\n147\t1. **Promotion rule.** You have 0 project-level skills. New skills start in\n148\t   `<project>/.claude/skills/` and only move to global once they prove out across\n149\t   2+ projects. See `project_skill-bloat-and-promotion`.\n150\t2. **Plugin side.** ~37 plugin skills load per session from 6 enabled plugins. That is\n151\t   a separate, smaller pass — done by disabling whole plugins, not individual skills.\n152\t   (The 606 SKILL.md files in `~/.claude/plugins/cache/` are mostly disabled plugins and\n153\t   cached old versions. They are disk clutter, not context cost.)\n154\t3. **Put it on a clock.** Monthly skill audit, weekly rules review. The decay table\n155\t   is in the companion note."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"66fcbdf9-0215-4fbd-aaa0-c5231a04b9ed","timestamp":"2026-09-12T14:05:26.213Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-skill-audit.md changed on disk since you last read it. That's usually deliberate, so take it as the current state rather than reverting it; if the change looks wrong, say so rather than undoing it yourself — otherwise no need to call it out. Here are the relevant changes (shown with line numbers):\n1\t---\n2\ttitle: \"Skill audit 2026-09-12 — 160 global skills down to 60\"\n3\tdate: 2026-09-12\n4\ttype: audit\n5\ttrigger: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6LNlCpQPYFc\n6\trelated: 2026-09-12-harness-over-model-mark-kashef.md\n7\tscript: ~/.claude/skills-audit-2026-09-12.sh\n8\ttags: [skills, context-engineering, claude-code, audit]\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t# Skill audit — 2026-09-12\n12\t\n13\tTriggered by the Mark Kashef / Nate Herk harness video. Companion note:\n14\t`2026-09-12-harness-over-model-mark-kashef.md`.\n15\t\n16\t## The bill\n17\t\n18\t| | |\n19\t|---|---|\n20\t| Global skills in `~/.claude/skills/` | **160** |\n21\t| — of which are symlinks into `~/.agents/skills/` | 45 |\n22\t| Plugin skills exposed per session | ~37 (6 plugins enabled, 18 disabled) |\n23\t| SKILL.md files in plugin cache | 606 — mostly disabled plugins and old versions, **not** the load |\n24\t| Project-level skills anywhere in `~/DEV` | **0** |\n25\t| Distinct skills ever invoked (414 sessions) | **58** |\n26\t| Used 3+ times | 30 |\n27\t| Never used at all | **130** |\n28\t| **Context cost of the skill roster, every session** | **~19,158 tokens** |\n29\t\n30\tThat last row is the real cost. Every skill's name and description is loaded into\n31\tcontext at session start whether you use it or not. 19k tokens is the standing tax.\n32\t\n33\t## The rule applied\n34\t\n35\tThe video's audit question is not *\"how often did I run this?\"* It is:\n36\t\n37\t> **\"Would the model do this fine without the skill now?\"**\n38\t\n39\tA skill is a crutch. As the brain gets smarter, the crutch starts to limit it.\n40\tSo the keep/cut split below is by **category**, not by usage count.\n41\t\n42\t## Verified before cutting\n43\t\n44\t- `interceptor`, `firecrawl`, `playwright`, `bright-data`, `apify` — **all missing\n45\t  from PATH.** Those skills can never fire successfully. Pure dead weight.\n46\t- **45 skills are symlinks** into `~/.agents/skills/`, the folder Codex and Cowork\n47\t  also read. Archiving a symlink de-registers it from Claude Code and **leaves the\n48\t  real skill intact for Codex.** That is the desired behaviour, not a side effect.\n49\t  (Related: `project_vendored-skill-install`, `project_vendored-skill-repair`.)\n50\t- 13 skills were installed in the last 14 days. They get a grace period — too new\n51\t  to judge. Not in any cut tier.\n52\t\n53\t## The tiers\n54\t\n55\t### Tier 1 — dead weight (43 skills, ~6,663 tokens)\n56\t\n57\tHighest confidence. No judgment calls.\n58\t\n59\t- **Broken tools (9):** `Interceptor` `Browser` `playwright-cli` `firecrawl`\n60\t  `firecrawl-build-onboarding` `BrightData` `Apify` `web-scraping` `everything-search`\n61\t  — binaries not installed.\n62\t- **Generic thinking / prompt skills (21):** `caveman` `zoom-out` `roast` `BeCreative`\n63\t  `Ideate` `FirstPrinciples` `SystemsThinking` `Aphorisms` `Prompting` `BiasCheck`\n64\t  `IterativeDepth` `ApertureOscillation` `BitterPillEngineering` `ExtractWisdom`\n65\t  `Council` `grill-me` `grill-with-docs` `backtrack` `fact-checker` `Fabric`\n66\t  — this is *exactly* the category the video says to delete. Opus 5 does these natively.\n67\t- **Not your domain (13):** `pp-flight-goat` `pp-movie-goat` `pp-recipe-goat` `Sales`\n68\t  `starter-icp-positioning` `starter-voice-profile` `USMetrics` `WorldThreatModel`\n69\t  `RedTeam` `PrivateInvestigator` `Art` `AudioEditor` `Remotion` `WriteStory`\n70\t\n71\t### Tier 2 — superseded (57 skills, ~6,137 tokens)\n72\t\n73\tYou already use something that covers each of these. Contains judgment calls —\n74\treview before running.\n75\t\n76\t- **Skill authoring** — `CreateSkill` (22 uses) covers: `write-a-skill`\n77\t  `writing-great-skills` `skill-evaluator` `find-skills`\n78\t- **Matt Pocock duplicates** — you invoke the `mattpocock-skills:*` plugin versions;\n79\t  these are stale local copies: `ask-matt` `setup-matt-pocock-skills` `codebase-design`\n80\t  `diagnosing-bugs` `domain-modeling` `tdd` `research`\n81\t- **Design pile** — `impeccable` (6) + `artifact-design` (8) cover: `gpt-taste`\n82\t  `high-end-visual-design` `image-to-code` `imagegen-frontend-*` `industrial-brutalist-ui`\n83\t  `minimalist-ui` `redesign-existing-projects` `stitch-design-taste` `design-academy-skill`\n84\t  `design-taste-frontend-v1` `starter-design-tokens` `brandkit` `Webdesign`\n85\t  `frontend-slides` `visual-explainer` `diagram-generator`\n86\t- **Workflow pile** — `superpowers:*` + `claudex-*` cover: `to-prd` `to-spec` `to-tickets`\n87\t  `triage` `implement` `wayfinder` `improve-codebase-architecture` `RootCauseAnalysis`\n88\t  `loop-designer` `Loop` `Delegation` `Migrate` `Optimize` `Hardening` `decision-toolkit`\n89\t  `CreateCLI` `Agents` `Evals` `ContextSearch` `Daemon` `LocalIntelligence` `Knowledge`\n90\t  `Interview` `process-interviewer` `research-assistant` `storm-research`\n91\t  `full-output-enforcement` `fable-mode` `Telos`\n92\t\n93\t### Keep — tacit knowledge, even at zero uses\n94\t\n95\tThese carry knowledge the model can never have. The video is explicit that this is\n96\tthe one category worth keeping forever.\n97\t\n98\t`game-film-timestamps` (Hudl/DVSport/ESPN timestamp repair) ·\n99\t`pp-catapult-openfield-connect` (GPS) · `pp-zotero-research-library` ·\n100\t`five-slides-generator` (your Baylor deck format) · `pp-fireflies`\n101\t\n102\t### Grace period — installed in the last 14 days\n103\t\n104\t`new-project` `brain-audit` `codex-build` `claudex-route` `claudex-loop`\n105\t`next-decision` `decisions` `before-building` `anti-sleep` `persistent-localhost`\n106\t`session-miner` `improve-system` `design-taste-frontend-v1`\n107\t\n108\t### Your call — not cut, not defended\n109\t\n110\t`pbir-report-builder` (Power BI / IBCS — plausibly useful for Baylor reporting,\n111\tunused 47 days) · `Science` · `ArXiv` · `pp-espn` · `weekly-review` ·\n112\t`ingest-resource` · `library-advisor` · `library-review` · `capture-insight` ·\n113\t`scroll-craft`\n114\t\n115\t## Result\n116\t\n117\t| Stage | Skills | Roster cost | Per-session tokens |\n118\t|---|---|---|---|\n119\t| Now | 160 | 76,632 ch | ~19,158 |\n120\t| After Tier 1 | 117 | 49,980 ch | ~12,495 |\n121\t| After Tier 2 | **60** | 25,430 ch | **~6,357** |\n122\t\n123\t**~12,800 tokens back every session — a 66% cut.**\n124\t\n125\t## How to run it\n126\t\n127\tThe script **archives, never deletes.** Everything moves to\n128\t`~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12/`.\n129\t\n130\t```bash\n131\tbash ~/.claude/skills-audit-2026-09-12.sh dryrun   # show, move nothing\n132\tbash ~/.claude/skills-audit-2026-09-12.sh tier1\n133\tbash ~/.claude/skills-audit-2026-09-12.sh tier2\n134\t```\n135\t\n136\tUndo, any time:\n137\t\n138\t```bash\n139\tmv ~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12/<name> ~/.claude/skills/   # one skill\n140\tmv ~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12/* ~/.claude/skills/        # all of them\n141\t```\n142\t\n143\tThen `/reload-skills` or start a new session.\n144\t\n145\t## Next steps after this\n146\t\n147\t1. **Promotion rule.** You have 0 project-level skills. New skills start in\n148\t   `<project>/.claude/skills/` and only move to global once they prove out across\n149\t   2+ projects. See `project_skill-bloat-and-promotion`.\n150\t2. **Plugin side.** ~37 plugin skills load per session from 6 enabled plugins. That is\n151\t   a separate, smaller pass — done by disabling whole plugins, not individual skills.\n152\t   (The 606 SKILL.md files in `~/.claude/plugins/cache/` are mostly disabled plugins and\n153\t   cached old versions. They are disk clutter, not context cost.)\n154\t3. **Put it on a clock.** Monthly skill audit, weekly rules review. The decay table\n155\t   is in the companion note.\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"66fcbdf9-0215-4fbd-aaa0-c5231a04b9ed","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/memory/project_skill-bloat-and-promotion.md","snippet":"1\t---\n2\tname: skill-bloat-and-promotion\n3\tdescription: \"Measured skill bloat in this setup, the project-until-promoted rule that fixes it, and the command that re-measures usage from .jsonl session logs\"\n4\tmetadata: \n5\t  node_type: memory\n6\t  type: project\n7\t  originSessionId: f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d\n8\t  modified: 2026-09-12T13:55:33.554Z\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\tMeasured 2026-09-12 from a gap analysis of a Nate Herk / **Mark Kashef** video\n12\t(\"How to Actually Choose the Right AI Agent\", 2026-09-11). Full note:\n13\t`~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-harness-over-model-mark-kashef.md`.\n14\tSame shape as the earlier audit in [[brain-audit-loop]] — that one scored the\n15\t**vault**, this one scores the **skill layer**.\n16\t\n17\t## The measurement\n18\t\n19\t| Metric | Count |\n20\t|---|---|\n21\t| Global skills in `~/.claude/skills/` | 164 |\n22\t| Plugin skills exposed per session | ~37 (6 enabled, 18 disabled) |\n23\t| SKILL.md files in plugin cache | 606 — disabled plugins + old versions, NOT the load |\n24\t| Exposed to one session's roster | ~120 |\n25\t| Project-level skills anywhere in `~/DEV` | **0** |\n26\t| `.jsonl` sessions scanned | 414 |\n27\t| Distinct real skills **ever** invoked | **58** |\n28\t| Used 3+ times | 30 |\n29\t| Used exactly once | 19 |\n30\t\n31\tEverything is global. That is the anti-pattern, not the achievement.\n32\t\n33\t## The rule that fixes it — promotion\n34\t\n35\t**Everything stays project-level until it earns global.** A skill lives in\n36\t`<project>/.claude/skills/` until it proves itself across 2+ projects, then it\n37\tgets promoted to `~/.claude/skills/`. Keeps the global load small and the blast\n38\tradius tight — so when something underperforms you can tell a *model* problem\n39\tfrom a *harness* problem from an *organisation* problem.\n40\t\n41\t## The audit question\n42\t\n43\tNot \"how often did I run this skill?\" but **\"would the model do this fine without\n44\tthe skill now?\"** A skill is a crutch; as the brain gets smarter the crutch starts\n45\tto *limit* it. Seasonal skills (`forecast-capture`) and occasional ones\n46\t(`ingest-book`) are low-count by design — low usage alone is not a delete signal.\n47\t\n48\t## Re-measure (BUILTIN filter is the whole trick)\n49\t\n50\tWithout the `BUILTIN` set you also count `/exit`, `/clear`, `/compact` and friends,\n51\twhich inflates the total to 74 and buries the real skills.\n52\t\n53\t```bash\n54\tpython3 - <<'EOF'\n55\timport glob,re,collections\n56\tBUILTIN={'exit','clear','compact','rename','color','login','logout','plugin','doctor',\n57\t'help','model','config','resume','cost','status','init','agents','mcp','hooks','memory',\n58\t'review','vim','terminal-setup','install-github-app','add-dir','bug','release-notes',\n59\t'pr-comments','ide','permissions','statusline','output-style','todos','export',\n60\t'privacy-settings','upgrade','fast','artifacts','tasks','loop','context','usage','rewind',\n61\t'sandbox','feedback','worktrees','skills','reload-skills','reload-plugins','bashes',\n62\t'keybindings','theme'}\n63\tc=collections.Counter()\n64\tfor f in glob.glob('/Users/kristinarash/.claude/projects/**/*.jsonl',recursive=True):\n65\t    try:\n66\t        for line in open(f,encoding='utf-8',errors='ignore'):\n67\t            if 'Skill' not in line and 'command-name' not in line: continue\n68\t            for m in re.finditer(r'\"name\"\\s*:\\s*\"Skill\"\\s*,\\s*\"input\"\\s*:\\s*\\{[^}]*?\"skill\"\\s*:\\s*\"([^\"]+)\"', line):\n69\t                c[m.group(1)]+=1\n70\t            for m in re.finditer(r'<command-name>/([a-zA-Z0-9:_-]+)</command-name>', line):\n71\t                c[m.group(1)]+=1\n72\t    except Exception: pass\n73\treal={k:v for k,v in c.items() if k.split(':')[-1] not in BUILTIN and k not in BUILTIN}\n74\tprint(\"distinct real skills ever invoked:\",len(real))\n75\tprint(\"used >=3 times:\",len([k for k,v in real.items() if v>=3]))\n76\tprint(\"used exactly once:\",len([k for k,v in real.items() if v==1]))\n77\tfor k,v in sorted(real.items(),key=lambda x:-x[1]): print(f\"{v:5d}  {k}\")\n78\tEOF\n79\t```\n80\t\n81\t## Decision, 2026-09-12\n82\t\n83\t**Do the skill audit BEFORE adopting Pi** (`pi.dev`) as an alternative harness.\n84\tRationale: the leverage is the bloat, not the harness. Auditing first means any\n85\tfuture harness move ports ~30 good skills instead of ~200 mixed ones.\n86\t\n87\tTwo Pi facts that are easy to get wrong: Pi ships **no permission prompts**\n88\t(confirmation flows are your extension's job — so never run it against `~/DEV` or\n89\tanything touching `DexaProject`), and **skills port but hooks do not** — the 61\n90\thooks in `~/.claude/hooks/` are Claude-Code-shaped and would be rewritten as Pi\n91\textensions. See also [[hook-two-copies-trap]] and [[dcg-guard-blocks]]."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"f4c8285d-52f8-465e-ac2c-65c882bfacf7","timestamp":"2026-09-12T14:05:26.216Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /Users/kristinarash/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/memory/project_skill-bloat-and-promotion.md changed on disk since you last read it. That's usually deliberate, so take it as the current state rather than reverting it; if the change looks wrong, say so rather than undoing it yourself — otherwise no need to call it out. Here are the relevant changes (shown with line numbers):\n1\t---\n2\tname: skill-bloat-and-promotion\n3\tdescription: \"Measured skill bloat in this setup, the project-until-promoted rule that fixes it, and the command that re-measures usage from .jsonl session logs\"\n4\tmetadata: \n5\t  node_type: memory\n6\t  type: project\n7\t  originSessionId: f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d\n8\t  modified: 2026-09-12T13:55:33.554Z\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\tMeasured 2026-09-12 from a gap analysis of a Nate Herk / **Mark Kashef** video\n12\t(\"How to Actually Choose the Right AI Agent\", 2026-09-11). Full note:\n13\t`~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-harness-over-model-mark-kashef.md`.\n14\tSame shape as the earlier audit in [[brain-audit-loop]] — that one scored the\n15\t**vault**, this one scores the **skill layer**.\n16\t\n17\t## The measurement\n18\t\n19\t| Metric | Count |\n20\t|---|---|\n21\t| Global skills in `~/.claude/skills/` | 164 |\n22\t| Plugin skills exposed per session | ~37 (6 enabled, 18 disabled) |\n23\t| SKILL.md files in plugin cache | 606 — disabled plugins + old versions, NOT the load |\n24\t| Exposed to one session's roster | ~120 |\n25\t| Project-level skills anywhere in `~/DEV` | **0** |\n26\t| `.jsonl` sessions scanned | 414 |\n27\t| Distinct real skills **ever** invoked | **58** |\n28\t| Used 3+ times | 30 |\n29\t| Used exactly once | 19 |\n30\t\n31\tEverything is global. That is the anti-pattern, not the achievement.\n32\t\n33\t## The rule that fixes it — promotion\n34\t\n35\t**Everything stays project-level until it earns global.** A skill lives in\n36\t`<project>/.claude/skills/` until it proves itself across 2+ projects, then it\n37\tgets promoted to `~/.claude/skills/`. Keeps the global load small and the blast\n38\tradius tight — so when something underperforms you can tell a *model* problem\n39\tfrom a *harness* problem from an *organisation* problem.\n40\t\n41\t## The audit question\n42\t\n43\tNot \"how often did I run this skill?\" but **\"would the model do this fine without\n44\tthe skill now?\"** A skill is a crutch; as the brain gets smarter the crutch starts\n45\tto *limit* it. Seasonal skills (`forecast-capture`) and occasional ones\n46\t(`ingest-book`) are low-count by design — low usage alone is not a delete signal.\n47\t\n48\t## Re-measure (BUILTIN filter is the whole trick)\n49\t\n50\tWithout the `BUILTIN` set you also count `/exit`, `/clear`, `/compact` and friends,\n51\twhich inflates the total to 74 and buries the real skills.\n52\t\n53\t```bash\n54\tpython3 - <<'EOF'\n55\timport glob,re,collections\n56\tBUILTIN={'exit','clear','compact','rename','color','login','logout','plugin','doctor',\n57\t'help','model','config','resume','cost','status','init','agents','mcp','hooks','memory',\n58\t'review','vim','terminal-setup','install-github-app','add-dir','bug','release-notes',\n59\t'pr-comments','ide','permissions','statusline','output-style','todos','export',\n60\t'privacy-settings','upgrade','fast','artifacts','tasks','loop','context','usage','rewind',\n61\t'sandbox','feedback','worktrees','skills','reload-skills','reload-plugins','bashes',\n62\t'keybindings','theme'}\n63\tc=collections.Counter()\n64\tfor f in glob.glob('/Users/kristinarash/.claude/projects/**/*.jsonl',recursive=True):\n65\t    try:\n66\t        for line in open(f,encoding='utf-8',errors='ignore'):\n67\t            if 'Skill' not in line and 'command-name' not in line: continue\n68\t            for m in re.finditer(r'\"name\"\\s*:\\s*\"Skill\"\\s*,\\s*\"input\"\\s*:\\s*\\{[^}]*?\"skill\"\\s*:\\s*\"([^\"]+)\"', line):\n69\t                c[m.group(1)]+=1\n70\t            for m in re.finditer(r'<command-name>/([a-zA-Z0-9:_-]+)</command-name>', line):\n71\t                c[m.group(1)]+=1\n72\t    except Exception: pass\n73\treal={k:v for k,v in c.items() if k.split(':')[-1] not in BUILTIN and k not in BUILTIN}\n74\tprint(\"distinct real skills ever invoked:\",len(real))\n75\tprint(\"used >=3 times:\",len([k for k,v in real.items() if v>=3]))\n76\tprint(\"used exactly once:\",len([k for k,v in real.items() if v==1]))\n77\tfor k,v in sorted(real.items(),key=lambda x:-x[1]): print(f\"{v:5d}  {k}\")\n78\tEOF\n79\t```\n80\t\n81\t## Decision, 2026-09-12\n82\t\n83\t**Do the skill audit BEFORE adopting Pi** (`pi.dev`) as an alternative harness.\n84\tRationale: the leverage is the bloat, not the harness. Auditing first means any\n85\tfuture harness move ports ~30 good skills instead of ~200 mixed ones.\n86\t\n87\tTwo Pi facts that are easy to get wrong: Pi ships **no permission prompts**\n88\t(confirmation flows are your extension's job — so never run it against `~/DEV` or\n89\tanything touching `DexaProject`), and **skills port but hooks do not** — the 61\n90\thooks in `~/.claude/hooks/` are Claude-Code-shaped and would be rewritten as Pi\n91\textensions. See also [[hook-two-copies-trap]] and [[dcg-guard-blocks]].\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"f4c8285d-52f8-465e-ac2c-65c882bfacf7","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-harness-over-model-mark-kashef.md","snippet":"1\t---\n2\ttitle: \"Harness over model — Mark Kashef on Nate Herk (video breakdown + gap analysis)\"\n3\tdate: 2026-09-12\n4\ttype: video-note\n5\tsource: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6LNlCpQPYFc\n6\tvideo_title: \"How to Actually Choose the Right AI Agent\"\n7\tchannel: Nate Herk | AI Automation\n8\tguest: Mark Kashef (Prompt Advisors)\n9\tpublished: 2026-09-11\n10\tduration: 34:08\n11\ttags: [ai-harness, claude-code, codex, pi, skills, aios, context-engineering]\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t# Harness over model\n15\t\n16\tVideo: **\"How to Actually Choose the Right AI Agent\"** — Nate Herk with **Mark Kashef**, 34 min, posted 2026-09-11.\n17\t\n18\t## The one idea\n19\t\n20\t> \"The harness of a model is much more important than the model itself.\" — Kashef, 0:06\n21\t\n22\tThe model is a **brain in a jar**. It takes input, gives output. That is all.\n23\tEverything that makes it useful — read, write, edit, bash, grep, the agentic loop —\n24\tis the **harness** wrapped around it.\n25\t\n26\tAt 2:20 the whiteboard shows this literally: a detailed factory drawing with a glowing\n27\tbrain in a jar at the dead centre, surrounded by machinery (books = read, press = write,\n28\tgears = bash, robot arms = tools). Two boxes at the bottom are crossed out — the things\n29\ta bare brain cannot do.\n30\t\n31\t**Proof demo (7:19–8:55):** Kashef opens LM Studio with a local Qwen model (captions read\n32\t\"Quen 27B\" — likely a 7B-class Qwen, too small on screen to confirm) — a raw brain with no\n33\tharness — and asks it to build a landing page *and spin up a local server*.\n34\tIt writes the HTML. It cannot start the server. No limbs.\n35\tGive the same prompt to Claude Code or Codex and it does not blink.\n36\t\n37\t**The workflow prediction that follows (3:25–3:41):** open-source local models already do\n38\t\"like 80% of the day-to-day work.\" He expects a world within a year where you run\n39\t**70–80% locally** (given the hardware) and only \"bring in the geniuses for genius-level\n40\ttasks or planning.\" The harness is what makes that split possible — the local brain gets\n41\tthe same limbs as the expensive one.\n42\t\n43\t**The consequence for benchmarks (20:29–21:45):** after the March 2026 Claude Code harness\n44\tleak, Kashef read the whole thing. Most of it was *crutches* — regex lists of swear words,\n45\tbehaviour guards, scaffolding. His takeaway:\n46\t\n47\t> \"There is a whole factory of workers making this model look way smarter than it is.\"\n48\t\n49\tSo stop being impressed by benchmark scores. Ask instead how well the provider improved\n50\t**the harness** to match the new brain. Judge empirically, on your own tasks.\n51\t\n52\t---\n53\t\n54\t## Chapter map\n55\t\n56\t| Time | Topic |\n57\t|---|---|\n58\t| 0:00 | Intro |\n59\t| 2:05 | Models vs. harnesses (the brain-in-a-jar diagram) |\n60\t| 7:19 | Local models in LM Studio — the no-harness proof |\n61\t| 9:26 | Sponsor (skip) |\n62\t| 10:29 | Choosing and switching AI agents |\n63\t| 13:37 | Hermes, OpenClaw, and **Pi** |\n64\t| 16:47 | Making your skills portable (`/poly-skill`) |\n65\t| 19:17 | Understanding your AI system |\n66\t| 21:46 | Keeping your AI OS updated (`rot.md`) |\n67\t| 24:14 | When models outgrow your skills |\n68\t| 30:01 | Organizing projects and global rules (**Promotion**) |\n69\t| 33:37 | Final thoughts |\n70\t\n71\t---\n72\t\n73\t## Hermes, OpenClaw, and Pi — the same category (13:37–15:28)\n74\t\n75\tAll three are **bring-your-own-model harnesses**: you supply the harness, then loop in\n76\twhatever brain you want.\n77\t\n78\t- **Hermes beat OpenClaw on the harness, not the skills.** Kashef is blunt at 14:14 —\n79\t  \"all these skill MD files, etc., they're all great, but the thing that made Hermes\n80\t  better than OpenClaw is its harness.\"\n81\t- **Vanilla Hermes loses to Codex.** Run the same task one-to-one, out of the box, and\n82\t  you get a different result. But you can *massage* Hermes's harness until it performs\n83\t  the same verification loop that makes Codex feel sharp. Monkey see, monkey do.\n84\t- **That is why everyone's Hermes agent is a \"special snowflake\"** (14:00) — people keep\n85\t  hacking the harness to fit how they want to use their models.\n86\t- **Pi is the vanilla open-source version** of the same idea (6:03), where you build your\n87\t  own harness from a minimal base.\n88\t\n89\tThe endgame he wants (15:34): one harness for everything, with models as a **leased brain**\n90\tyou swap out as needed. His framing at 15:47 — the ecosystem is tribal right now\n91\t(\"I am team Codex, I am team Claude Code, you are all Neanderthals\"). He is deliberately\n92\tanti-tribal: build so that whichever brain is best, fastest, or cheapest today can be\n93\tswapped in with near-zero acclimation. If Gemini \"truly wakes up\" tomorrow, he wants the\n94\tswap to cost 24 hours, not a rebuild.\n95\t\n96\t---\n97\t\n98\t## Gap analysis — video claim vs. your actual setup\n99\t\n100\t| # | Technique (timestamp) | What the video says | Your status | Your equivalent |\n101\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n102\t| 1 | **Zero provider loyalty** (11:13) | Be loyal to your harness and assets, never the model. Swap brains freely. | **Have** | `claudex-route`, `claudex-loop` |\n103\t| 2 | **Claude plans, Codex executes** (12:14) | Claude Code = gifted artist / wise old owl. Codex = surgeon / rottweiler. Make them fight in a loop for ~10 rounds until Claude has what Codex saw. | **Have** | `claudex-loop`, `codex-review`, `codex-build` |\n104\t| 3 | **`/poly-skill` — dual-format skills** (16:47) | Feed Claude *and* Codex docs to an agent, have it write one skill that both pick up reliably. Codex prioritises different fields than Claude. Python is Python; the *invocation* needs massaging. | **Partial** | `~/.claude/AGENTS.md` sibling exists, `CreateSkill` exists — but your 164 skills are Claude-shaped only |\n105\t| 4 | **Monthly skill audit on cron** (18:40) | Auto-audit the whole ecosystem monthly: cut unused skills, merge overlapping ones into compound skills, re-check model-agnosticism. | **Missing** | `doctor-plus` is manual only |\n106\t| 5 | **`rot.md` — decay tiers** (22:41) | One file that records how fast each AIOS layer rots, so you audit on the right clock. | **Missing** | — |\n107\t| 6 | **Delete skills aggressively** (24:14) | *Per the video,* Boris Cherny tweeted you should delete all your skills every 6 months. A skill is a crutch. If the model outgrew it, the skill now *limits* it. | **Missing** | — |\n108\t| 7 | **Promotion rule** (31:32) | Everything is project-level until it **earns** global. Few global things. Small blast radius. | **Missing — and inverted** | You have **0** project-level skills. Everything is global. |\n109\t| 8 | **Many small OSes, not one mega** (30:19) | ~25 isolated operating systems (tax, consulting, school, enterprise). One meta-system audits them all. Isolation = you can tell model problem from harness problem from organisation problem. | **Partial** | `~/DEV` is one workspace; 23 projects carry their own `CLAUDE.md` |\n110\t| 9 | **Mine your own JSONL logs** (15:04) | Every Claude/Codex conversation lives on disk with tool-call metadata. Read them to reverse-engineer what a good harness does — \"monkey see, monkey do\". | **Missing** | You have **414** `.jsonl` sessions sitting unused |\n111\t| 10 | **Own your harness (Pi)** (14:39) | pi.dev — copy an open-source harness onto your machine, then shape it until it behaves like the bits of Claude Code and Codex you love. | **Missing** | `pi` not installed |\n112\t| 11 | **Ambitious prompts** (29:02) | Anthropic's own release notes say *give it more ambitious tasks*. Kashef prompts \"build this, but impress me.\" | **Missing** | — |\n113\t\n114\t### The decay tiers (22:41–24:14) — copy this table\n115\t\n116\t| Layer | How often it rots | Audit every |\n117\t|---|---|---|\n118\t| Identity (who you are, goals) | Very slow | 1–3 months |\n119\t| Substrate / core context | Slow | 1–3 months |\n120\t| Skills | **Fast** | Monthly |\n121\t| Agents | **Fast** | Monthly |\n122\t| Rules | **Fastest** | Weekly (Kashef changes some daily) |\n123\t| Hooks | Slow | ~6 months |\n124\t\n125\tHis reasoning on rules: every new task with the same AIOS exposes a new edge case,\n126\tso rules get rewritten constantly. Skills get *deleted*, rules get *sharpened*.\n127\t\n128\t### Why skills rot (24:26–27:38)\n129\t\n\n... [241 lines truncated] ..."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"9f622157-3dcb-4093-886b-22cf50d78264","timestamp":"2026-09-12T14:05:26.217Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-harness-over-model-mark-kashef.md changed on disk since you last read it. That's usually deliberate, so take it as the current state rather than reverting it; if the change looks wrong, say so rather than undoing it yourself — otherwise no need to call it out. Here are the relevant changes (shown with line numbers):\n1\t---\n2\ttitle: \"Harness over model — Mark Kashef on Nate Herk (video breakdown + gap analysis)\"\n3\tdate: 2026-09-12\n4\ttype: video-note\n5\tsource: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6LNlCpQPYFc\n6\tvideo_title: \"How to Actually Choose the Right AI Agent\"\n7\tchannel: Nate Herk | AI Automation\n8\tguest: Mark Kashef (Prompt Advisors)\n9\tpublished: 2026-09-11\n10\tduration: 34:08\n11\ttags: [ai-harness, claude-code, codex, pi, skills, aios, context-engineering]\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t# Harness over model\n15\t\n16\tVideo: **\"How to Actually Choose the Right AI Agent\"** — Nate Herk with **Mark Kashef**, 34 min, posted 2026-09-11.\n17\t\n18\t## The one idea\n19\t\n20\t> \"The harness of a model is much more important than the model itself.\" — Kashef, 0:06\n21\t\n22\tThe model is a **