{"parentUuid":"2fd9f002-5142-454b-abb8-290ffb04ba44","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"0c142cad-0b6f-4d85-a335-7763a49aa9c2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KfZjsr2k7scyopsbGKFDQF","type":"tool_result","content":"# Pi Skills: Architecture & Usage\n\n## File Format & Location\n\nSkills use YAML frontmatter with `name` and `description` fields in a `SKILL.md` file within a skill directory:\n\n```\nmy-skill/\n├── SKILL.md\n├── scripts/\n└── assets/\n```\n\n**SKILL.md example:**\n```markdown\n---\nname: my-skill\ndescription: What this skill does and when to use it.\n---\n# My Skill\n```\n\nDiscovery paths include:\n- Global: `~/.pi/agent/skills/`, `~/.agents/skills/`\n- Project: `.pi/skills/`, `.agents/skills/`\n- CLI: `--skill <path>`\n\n## Invocation\n\nSkills support both automatic and manual invocation:\n\n**Automatic:** The system prompt includes skill descriptions; agents invoke them contextually.\n\n**Manual:** Use slash commands like `/skill:brave-search` or `/skill:pdf-tools extract`.\n\nArguments append as: `User: <args>`\n\n## Progressive Disclosure\n\nAs stated: \"only descriptions are always in context, full instructions load on-demand.\" The agent reads `SKILL.md` when the task matches the skill's purpose.\n\n## Claude Code Compatibility\n\nYes—Pi implements the Agent Skills standard, and Claude Code skills can be reused. Add Claude Code skill directories to Pi's settings:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"skills\": [\"~/.claude/skills\"]\n}\n```\n\nPi permits skill names to differ from parent directories, making shared repositories viable across multiple harnesses."}]},"uuid":"37eeb0e6-35e1-4470-a6f5-077160e1eef1","timestamp":"2026-09-12T14:22:13.469Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":7363,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Pi Skills: Architecture & Usage\n\n## File Format & Location\n\nSkills use YAML frontmatter with `name` and `description` fields in a `SKILL.md` file within a skill directory:\n\n```\nmy-skill/\n├── SKILL.md\n├── scripts/\n└── assets/\n```\n\n**SKILL.md example:**\n```markdown\n---\nname: my-skill\ndescription: What this skill does and when to use it.\n---\n# My Skill\n```\n\nDiscovery paths include:\n- Global: `~/.pi/agent/skills/`, `~/.agents/skills/`\n- Project: `.pi/skills/`, `.agents/skills/`\n- CLI: `--skill <path>`\n\n## Invocation\n\nSkills support both automatic and manual invocation:\n\n**Automatic:** The system prompt includes skill descriptions; agents invoke them contextually.\n\n**Manual:** Use slash commands like `/skill:brave-search` or `/skill:pdf-tools extract`.\n\nArguments append as: `User: <args>`\n\n## Progressive Disclosure\n\nAs stated: \"only descriptions are always in context, full instructions load on-demand.\" The agent reads `SKILL.md` when the task matches the skill's purpose.\n\n## Claude Code Compatibility\n\nYes—Pi implements the Agent Skills standard, and Claude Code skills can be reused. Add Claude Code skill directories to Pi's settings:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"skills\": [\"~/.claude/skills\"]\n}\n```\n\nPi permits skill names to differ from parent directories, making shared repositories viable across multiple harnesses.","durationMs":5164,"url":"https://pi.dev/docs/latest/skills"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2fd9f002-5142-454b-abb8-290ffb04ba44","session_id":"f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"48954f0f-5658-4dee-ad81-6b7d8f52bdd4","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"0c142cad-0b6f-4d85-a335-7763a49aa9c2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0149mVQ9cU6n8ZVSgqae8Lwa","type":"tool_result","content":"# Pi's Security Model\n\n## Permission Model\nPi operates as a local agent with user permissions—no built-in approval flows exist. It \"runs with the permissions of the user account that starts it\" and treats user-writable files as trusted.\n\n## Project Trust\nProject trust is a resource-loading guard that controls whether pi loads project-local configurations. When pi detects protected resources (`.pi/settings.json`, extensions, skills, or system prompt files), it checks for a saved trust decision. The default behavior is `\"ask\"` when interactive, but non-interactive modes skip untrusted resources unless overridden.\n\nImportantly: \"Project trust is only an input-loading guard. It prevents a repository from silently changing pi's settings or extensions before you approve it.\"\n\n## No Sandbox Boundaries\nPi explicitly lacks isolation. The documentation emphasizes: \"Pi does not include a built-in sandbox. Built-in tools can read files, write files, edit files, and run shell commands with the permissions of the pi process.\"\n\nThe rationale is pragmatic—a partial sandbox creates false security assumptions while depending on host tools and credentials anyway.\n\n## Key Risks\nThe documentation warns that \"Prompt injection from repository files, comments, documentation, context files, or build output is expected local-agent risk and cannot be reliably prevented by pi.\"\n\n## Mitigation Strategy\nFor untrusted work, containerize pi: \"run the whole `pi` process inside a container/sandbox\" with minimal mounted paths and credentials, restricting network access when possible."}]},"uuid":"e95c8ce5-d965-4cb3-86e6-44065831ddaf","timestamp":"2026-09-12T14:22:16.793Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":5313,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Pi's Security Model\n\n## Permission Model\nPi operates as a local agent with user permissions—no built-in approval flows exist. It \"runs with the permissions of the user account that starts it\" and treats user-writable files as trusted.\n\n## Project Trust\nProject trust is a resource-loading guard that controls whether pi loads project-local configurations. When pi detects protected resources (`.pi/settings.json`, extensions, skills, or system prompt files), it checks for a saved trust decision. The default behavior is `\"ask\"` when interactive, but non-interactive modes skip untrusted resources unless overridden.\n\nImportantly: \"Project trust is only an input-loading guard. It prevents a repository from silently changing pi's settings or extensions before you approve it.\"\n\n## No Sandbox Boundaries\nPi explicitly lacks isolation. The documentation emphasizes: \"Pi does not include a built-in sandbox. Built-in tools can read files, write files, edit files, and run shell commands with the permissions of the pi process.\"\n\nThe rationale is pragmatic—a partial sandbox creates false security assumptions while depending on host tools and credentials anyway.\n\n## Key Risks\nThe documentation warns that \"Prompt injection from repository files, comments, documentation, context files, or build output is expected local-agent risk and cannot be reliably prevented by pi.\"\n\n## Mitigation Strategy\nFor untrusted work, containerize pi: \"run the whole `pi` process inside a container/sandbox\" with minimal mounted paths and credentials, restricting network access when possible.","durationMs":4810,"url":"https://pi.dev/docs/latest/security"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"48954f0f-5658-4dee-ad81-6b7d8f52bdd4","session_id":"f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"9be68d14-ae9e-4f7f-840e-747f35e207cd","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"0c142cad-0b6f-4d85-a335-7763a49aa9c2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E3YWkPiKjhmyyoohDWhQme","type":"tool_result","content":"# Pi Extensions Overview\n\n## Language & Location\n\nExtensions are **TypeScript modules** that extend Pi's behavior. They're auto-discovered from:\n- `~/.pi/agent/extensions/` (global)\n- `.pi/extensions/` (project-local)\n\nExtensions can be single files (`my-extension.ts`) or directories with `index.ts`.\n\n## Basic Structure\n\nAn extension exports a default factory function receiving the `ExtensionAPI`:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { ExtensionAPI } from \"@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\";\n\nexport default function (pi: ExtensionAPI) {\n  pi.on(\"session_start\", async (_event, ctx) => {\n    ctx.ui.notify(\"Extension loaded!\", \"info\");\n  });\n\n  pi.registerTool({\n    name: \"greet\",\n    description: \"Greet someone\",\n    parameters: Type.Object({\n      name: Type.String()\n    }),\n    async execute(toolCallId, params, signal, onUpdate, ctx) {\n      return {\n        content: [{ type: \"text\", text: `Hello, ${params.name}!` }],\n        details: {}\n      };\n    }\n  });\n}\n```\n\n## Key Capabilities\n\nExtensions can:\n- **Register custom tools** the LLM can invoke\n- **Register commands** like `/mycommand`\n- **Subscribe to lifecycle events** for interception and customization\n- **Interact with users** via prompts, dialogs, and custom UI\n- **Store persistent state** surviving restarts\n- **Control rendering** of tool calls and results\n\n## Lifecycle Events\n\nMajor events include:\n\n| Event | Purpose |\n|-------|---------|\n| `session_start` | Session initialized; setup resources |\n| `session_shutdown` | Cleanup before teardown |\n| `before_agent_start` | Inject context or modify system prompt |\n| `tool_call` | **Block or patch** tool invocations |\n| `tool_result` | **Modify** tool output before LLM receives it |\n| `input` | Intercept user input before expansion |\n| `model_select` | Model changed |\n| `message_start/end` | Message lifecycle hooks |\n\n## Event Handler Pattern\n\nHandlers receive `(event, ctx)` and can return values to modify behavior:\n\n```typescript\npi.on(\"tool_call\", async (event, ctx) => {\n  if (event.toolName === \"bash\" && event.input.command?.includes(\"rm -rf\")) {\n    const ok = await ctx.ui.confirm(\"Dangerous!\", \"Allow rm -rf?\");\n    if (!ok) return { block: true, reason: \"Blocked by user\" };\n  }\n});\n```\n\nExtensions run with full system permissions—only install from trusted sources."}]},"uuid":"cd2ac2ac-840b-4be4-ba1a-2e26a4a1e903","timestamp":"2026-09-12T14:22:17.947Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":124648,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Pi Extensions Overview\n\n## Language & Location\n\nExtensions are **TypeScript modules** that extend Pi's behavior. They're auto-discovered from:\n- `~/.pi/agent/extensions/` (global)\n- `.pi/extensions/` (project-local)\n\nExtensions can be single files (`my-extension.ts`) or directories with `index.ts`.\n\n## Basic Structure\n\nAn extension exports a default factory function receiving the `ExtensionAPI`:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { ExtensionAPI } from \"@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\";\n\nexport default function (pi: ExtensionAPI) {\n  pi.on(\"session_start\", async (_event, ctx) => {\n    ctx.ui.notify(\"Extension loaded!\", \"info\");\n  });\n\n  pi.registerTool({\n    name: \"greet\",\n    description: \"Greet someone\",\n    parameters: Type.Object({\n      name: Type.String()\n    }),\n    async execute(toolCallId, params, signal, onUpdate, ctx) {\n      return {\n        content: [{ type: \"text\", text: `Hello, ${params.name}!` }],\n        details: {}\n      };\n    }\n  });\n}\n```\n\n## Key Capabilities\n\nExtensions can:\n- **Register custom tools** the LLM can invoke\n- **Register commands** like `/mycommand`\n- **Subscribe to lifecycle events** for interception and customization\n- **Interact with users** via prompts, dialogs, and custom UI\n- **Store persistent state** surviving restarts\n- **Control rendering** of tool calls and results\n\n## Lifecycle Events\n\nMajor events include:\n\n| Event | Purpose |\n|-------|---------|\n| `session_start` | Session initialized; setup resources |\n| `session_shutdown` | Cleanup before teardown |\n| `before_agent_start` | Inject context or modify system prompt |\n| `tool_call` | **Block or patch** tool invocations |\n| `tool_result` | **Modify** tool output before LLM receives it |\n| `input` | Intercept user input before expansion |\n| `model_select` | Model changed |\n| `message_start/end` | Message lifecycle hooks |\n\n## Event Handler Pattern\n\nHandlers receive `(event, ctx)` and can return values to modify behavior:\n\n```typescript\npi.on(\"tool_call\", async (event, ctx) => {\n  if (event.toolName === \"bash\" && event.input.command?.includes(\"rm -rf\")) {\n    const ok = await ctx.ui.confirm(\"Dangerous!\", \"Allow rm -rf?\");\n    if (!ok) return { block: true, reason: \"Blocked by user\" };\n  }\n});\n```\n\nExtensions run with full system permissions—only install from trusted sources.","durationMs":8079,"url":"https://pi.dev/docs/latest/extensions"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9be68d14-ae9e-4f7f-840e-747f35e207cd","session_id":"f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"d2e2f4fd-fbc4-46a5-8b91-962471098ec1","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"0c142cad-0b6f-4d85-a335-7763a49aa9c2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RTZdq21iAtR8ozdur5Ck1e","type":"tool_result","content":"# Pi Installation and Setup Summary\n\n## Install Command\n```bash\nnpm install -g --ignore-scripts @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\n```\n\n## First Run\nAfter installation, navigate to your project and launch Pi:\n```bash\ncd /path/to/project\npi\n```\n\n## Authentication Options\n\n**Subscription-based:** Run `/login` within Pi and select providers like Claude Pro/Max or ChatGPT Plus.\n\n**API Key method:** Set environment variable before launching:\n```bash\nexport ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...\npi\n```\n\n## Directory Created\nPi stores configuration and sessions in `~/.pi/agent/`, which persists even after uninstalling the package.\n\n## Context Files Explained\n\n**AGENTS.md vs CLAUDE.md:** The documentation mentions Pi loads `\"AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md from parent directories and the current directory\"` for project-specific instructions—essentially interchangeable defaults for the same purpose.\n\n**AGENTS.override.md:** This file takes precedence, allowing you to override standard instructions when present in a directory.\n\n**Note:** The documentation does not mention a \"SYSTEM.md\" file; only AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, and AGENTS.override.md are referenced.\n\n## First Command\nOnce authenticated, try: `Summarize this repository and tell me how to run its checks.`"}]},"uuid":"ff5de7b1-87af-4049-a6af-79b0e7911ee4","timestamp":"2026-09-12T14:22:18.494Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":4871,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Pi Installation and Setup Summary\n\n## Install Command\n```bash\nnpm install -g --ignore-scripts @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\n```\n\n## First Run\nAfter installation, navigate to your project and launch Pi:\n```bash\ncd /path/to/project\npi\n```\n\n## Authentication Options\n\n**Subscription-based:** Run `/login` within Pi and select providers like Claude Pro/Max or ChatGPT Plus.\n\n**API Key method:** Set environment variable before launching:\n```bash\nexport ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...\npi\n```\n\n## Directory Created\nPi stores configuration and sessions in `~/.pi/agent/`, which persists even after uninstalling the package.\n\n## Context Files Explained\n\n**AGENTS.md vs CLAUDE.md:** The documentation mentions Pi loads `\"AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md from parent directories and the current directory\"` for project-specific instructions—essentially interchangeable defaults for the same purpose.\n\n**AGENTS.override.md:** This file takes precedence, allowing you to override standard instructions when present in a directory.\n\n**Note:** The documentation does not mention a \"SYSTEM.md\" file; only AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, and AGENTS.override.md are referenced.\n\n## First Command\nOnce authenticated, try: `Summarize this repository and tell me how to run its checks.`","durationMs":4951,"url":"https://pi.dev/docs/latest/quickstart"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d2e2f4fd-fbc4-46a5-8b91-962471098ec1","session_id":"f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main"}
