{"parentUuid":"8269158f-030f-417c-a5d5-7bb1287124ea","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeyhGdW9ewkhvAcQFyGHK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UK8LKPLbT3T2qVo25aRAkC","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://pi.dev/docs/latest/compaction","prompt":"How does Pi handle context compaction and sessions? Is compaction automatic or manual? How does the tree-structured session history and branching work? What commands control it?"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3574,"cache_read_input_tokens":217450,"output_tokens":1374,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":764},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3574,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1374,"cache_read_input_tokens":217450,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3574,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3574},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":5,"requestId":"req_011CeyhGchXiTHEGZSCyxpSK","type":"assistant","uuid":"3c5f138b-fb28-4862-a332-ca5ae734d96e","timestamp":"2026-09-12T14:22:36.736Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"d460571b-7d9e-45ef-94ba-90072eb31f38","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"0c142cad-0b6f-4d85-a335-7763a49aa9c2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0161NdcWSNkUdtiqGdwJojig","type":"tool_result","content":"# Running Pi in Containers and Sandboxes\n\nThere are four main approaches, each with different isolation and credential handling:\n\n## Quick Command Reference\n\n**Plain Docker** (simplest):\n```bash\ndocker build -t pi-sandbox -f Dockerfile.pi .\ndocker run --rm -it -e ANTHROPIC_API_KEY -v \"$PWD:/workspace\" pi-sandbox\n```\n\n**Docker Sandboxes** (credentials isolated):\n```bash\nsbx secret set-custom --host api.anthropic.com --env ANTHROPIC_OAUTH_TOKEN\nsbx run --kit \"docker.io/sbx/pi-kit:latest\" pi\n```\n\n**OpenShell** (policy-controlled):\n```bash\nopenshell sandbox create --name pi-sandbox --from pi -- pi\n```\n\n**Gondolin** (local micro-VM):\n```bash\npi -e ~/.pi/agent/extensions/gondolin\n```\n\n## Key Differences\n\n| Method | Credential Handling | Best For |\n|--------|-------------------|----------|\n| Plain Docker | Keys enter container | Simple local isolation |\n| Docker Sandboxes | \"sentinel value\" with proxy substitution | Host-side credential protection |\n| OpenShell | Gateway-managed injection | Remote/policy-controlled sandboxes |\n| Gondolin | Runs on host | Minimal isolation overhead |\n\n## What to Mount\n\n- **Workspace**: Mount your project directory (typically `/workspace` in containers)\n- **Agent home**: Consider using named volumes for container-local settings rather than exposing host auth files\n- **Credentials**: Only pass `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` via environment variables; Docker Sandboxes provides built-in credential isolation\n\nDocker Sandboxes offers the strongest credential model: \"the provider credential is not passed into the container\" but instead uses a proxy substitution mechanism on egress."}]},"uuid":"ec077bc6-6bb8-4ac6-9d16-014b96a7234c","timestamp":"2026-09-12T14:22:41.508Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":6868,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Running Pi in Containers and Sandboxes\n\nThere are four main approaches, each with different isolation and credential handling:\n\n## Quick Command Reference\n\n**Plain Docker** (simplest):\n```bash\ndocker build -t pi-sandbox -f Dockerfile.pi .\ndocker run --rm -it -e ANTHROPIC_API_KEY -v \"$PWD:/workspace\" pi-sandbox\n```\n\n**Docker Sandboxes** (credentials isolated):\n```bash\nsbx secret set-custom --host api.anthropic.com --env ANTHROPIC_OAUTH_TOKEN\nsbx run --kit \"docker.io/sbx/pi-kit:latest\" pi\n```\n\n**OpenShell** (policy-controlled):\n```bash\nopenshell sandbox create --name pi-sandbox --from pi -- pi\n```\n\n**Gondolin** (local micro-VM):\n```bash\npi -e ~/.pi/agent/extensions/gondolin\n```\n\n## Key Differences\n\n| Method | Credential Handling | Best For |\n|--------|-------------------|----------|\n| Plain Docker | Keys enter container | Simple local isolation |\n| Docker Sandboxes | \"sentinel value\" with proxy substitution | Host-side credential protection |\n| OpenShell | Gateway-managed injection | Remote/policy-controlled sandboxes |\n| Gondolin | Runs on host | Minimal isolation overhead |\n\n## What to Mount\n\n- **Workspace**: Mount your project directory (typically `/workspace` in containers)\n- **Agent home**: Consider using named volumes for container-local settings rather than exposing host auth files\n- **Credentials**: Only pass `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` via environment variables; Docker Sandboxes provides built-in credential isolation\n\nDocker Sandboxes offers the strongest credential model: \"the provider credential is not passed into the container\" but instead uses a proxy substitution mechanism on egress.","durationMs":5621,"url":"https://pi.dev/docs/latest/containerization"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d460571b-7d9e-45ef-94ba-90072eb31f38","session_id":"f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main"}
