{"parentUuid":"772b8e64-fbb0-4e12-a595-9a42ffe1af3d","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"0c142cad-0b6f-4d85-a335-7763a49aa9c2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VJJvzTgV1AypTh8zx26CvE","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-pi-harness-research.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"1875c456-2f0d-4f07-80c9-3dab2309fcff","timestamp":"2026-09-12T14:23:51.562Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-pi-harness-research.md","content":"---\ntitle: \"Pi (pi.dev) — harness research for a possible migration\"\ndate: 2026-09-12\ntype: research\nsource: https://pi.dev/docs/latest\nrelated: 2026-09-12-harness-over-model-mark-kashef.md\ntags: [pi, harness, agentic, claude-code, codex, migration]\n---\n\n# Pi — what it is, and what moving would actually cost\n\nResearched from the official docs at `pi.dev/docs/latest` on 2026-09-12.\nTriggered by the Mark Kashef harness video (`2026-09-12-harness-over-model-mark-kashef.md`),\nwhich spends about 60 seconds on Pi. This note is the part the video skipped.\n\n## One-paragraph summary\n\nPi is a **minimal terminal coding harness**, MIT licensed, from Earendil Inc. and\ncontributors. It ships seven tools — `read` `bash` `edit` `write` `grep` `find` `ls` —\nand nothing else. Everything beyond that (permission prompts included) is something\n**you** add through extensions, skills, prompt templates, themes, and packages. It\nspeaks to 30+ model providers and swaps between them mid-session. It implements the\nsame **Agent Skills standard** Claude Code uses, so your skill library is portable\nwith one line of config.\n\n## Install\n\n```bash\nnpm install -g --ignore-scripts @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\n# or\ncurl -fsSL https://pi.dev/install.sh | sh\n```\n\nYour `node` is v26.6.0 / `npm` 11.18.0 at `/opt/homebrew/bin/` — both work.\nPi stores everything in `~/.pi/agent/` (and that folder survives uninstalling the npm\npackage, so a trial leaves a trace you should clean up deliberately).\n\nFirst run:\n```bash\ncd /path/to/project\npi\n```\n\n## The three things that matter most for you\n\n### 1. Your skills port with ONE line — better than I first said\n\nPi implements the Agent Skills standard. Same `SKILL.md`, same YAML frontmatter with\n`name` and `description`, same progressive disclosure (\"only descriptions are always\nin context, full instructions load on-demand\").\n\nPi's skill discovery paths, out of the box:\n\n| Scope | Path |\n|---|---|\n| Global | `~/.pi/agent/skills/` and **`~/.agents/skills/`** |\n| Project | `.pi/skills/` and `.agents/skills/` |\n| One-off | `pi --skill <path>` |\n\n**`~/.agents/skills/` is already in that list.** That is the exact shared folder your\n45 symlinked skills point into. Pi would see them on day one with zero configuration.\n\nTo add the rest, one setting in `~/.pi/agent/settings.json`:\n\n```json\n{ \"skills\": [\"~/.claude/skills\"] }\n```\n\nPi also allows a skill's `name` to differ from its directory name, which is what makes\none shared repo workable across Claude Code, Codex and Pi at the same time.\n\n**Also portable:** Pi loads **`AGENTS.md` *or* `CLAUDE.md`** from the current and parent\ndirectories. Your context files move as-is. `AGENTS.override.md` takes precedence when\npresent — useful for a per-project override.\n\n> Correction to my earlier note: I said Pi uses \"AGENTS.md and SYSTEM.md\". The docs\n> pages mention only `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.override.md`. `SYSTEM.md`\n> appeared on the pi.dev homepage blurb but I could not confirm it in the docs.\n\n### 2. Your hooks become Extensions — a real port, but shorter than I said\n\nPi extensions are **TypeScript modules** in `~/.pi/agent/extensions/` (global) or\n`.pi/extensions/` (project). Your 61 hooks are *already* `.ts` files. The logic\nsurvives; the wrapper changes from \"standalone script reading stdin JSON\" to \"module\nexporting a factory that receives an `ExtensionAPI`\".\n\n```typescript\nimport type { ExtensionAPI } from \"@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\";\n\nexport default function (pi: ExtensionAPI) {\n  pi.on(\"session_start\", async (_event, ctx) => {\n    ctx.ui.notify(\"Extension loaded!\", \"info\");\n  });\n}\n```\n\nLifecycle events available:\n\n| Event | What it is for |\n|---|---|\n| `session_start` | Set up resources — your `keystone-unified-snapshot.js` equivalent |\n| `session_shutdown` | Cleanup — your closeout/backup hooks |\n| `before_agent_start` | **Inject context or modify the system prompt** — your memory injection |\n| `tool_call` | **Block or patch a tool call** — your `dcg` guard |\n| `tool_result` | Modify tool output before the model sees it |\n| `input` | Intercept user input before expansion |\n| `model_select`, `message_start/end` | Model and message lifecycle |\n\nThe docs' own `tool_call` example is *literally* your dcg guard:\n\n```typescript\npi.on(\"tool_call\", async (event, ctx) => {\n  if (event.toolName === \"bash\" && event.input.command?.includes(\"rm -rf\")) {\n    const ok = await ctx.ui.confirm(\"Dangerous!\", \"Allow rm -rf?\");\n    if (!ok) return { block: true, reason: \"Blocked by user\" };\n  }\n});\n```\n\nExtensions can also register tools the model can call, register `/commands`, show\ncustom UI, store state across restarts, and control how tool calls render.\n\n> Correction to my earlier note: I said porting hooks was \"weeks of work, not an\n> afternoon.\" That over-stated it. The event model maps cleanly onto what your hooks\n> already do, and they are already TypeScript. It is still one deliberate port per\n> hook — not free — but it is refactoring, not a rewrite in a new paradigm.\n\n### 3. The security model — the reason NOT to point it at `~/DEV`\n\nStraight from the docs, and stronger than the video suggests:\n\n> \"Pi does not include a built-in sandbox. Built-in tools can read files, write files,\n> edit files, and run shell commands with the permissions of the pi process.\"\n\n- **No built-in approval flows.** None. Claude Code's permission prompts, your `dcg`\n  guard, your backup default-deny — none of that exists until you build it as an\n  extension. This is a deliberate design choice, not an oversight: the docs argue a\n  partial sandbox creates false confidence.\n- **Project trust is only an input-loading guard.** It stops a repo from silently\n  changing Pi's settings or extensions before you approve. It does **not** restrict\n  what the agent can then do. Default is `\"ask\"` when interactive; non-interactive\n  modes skip untrusted resources.\n- **Prompt injection is accepted as unavoidable.** \"Prompt injection from repository\n  files, comments, documentation, context files, or build output is expected local-agent\n  risk and cannot be reliably prevented by pi.\"\n\n**Therefore:** never run Pi in `~/DEV`, and never anywhere near `DexaProject`\n(identifiable athlete health data, now on Google Drive). Trial it in a throwaway\ndirectory, or containerise it.\n\n## Containerising it — four documented paths\n\n| Method | Credentials | Best for |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Plain Docker** | Key enters the container | Simple local isolation |\n| **Docker Sandboxes** (`sbx`) | Sentinel value + proxy substitution — **key never enters the container** | Strongest credential protection |\n| **OpenShell** | Gateway-managed injection | Remote / policy-controlled |\n| **Gondolin** | Runs on host, micro-VM | Minimal isolation overhead |\n\n```bash\n# plain docker\ndocker build -t pi-sandbox -f Dockerfile.pi .\ndocker run --rm -it -e ANTHROPIC_API_KEY -v \"$PWD:/workspace\" pi-sandbox\n\n# docker sandboxes — credential stays on the host\nsbx secret set-custom --host api.anthropic.com --env ANTHROPIC_OAUTH_TOKEN\nsbx run --kit \"docker.io/sbx/pi-kit:latest\" pi\n```\n\n## Models — the anti-tribal promise, and its price tag\n\n30+ providers. Subscription-based: **Claude Pro/Max**, ChatGPT Plus/Pro, GitHub Copilot,\nxAI, OpenRouter, Radius. API-key: Anthropic, OpenAI, DeepSeek, Gemini, Azure, Bedrock,\nMistral, Groq, Cloudflare, and 20+ more.\n\n- Switch mid-session with **`/model`**. Local models via `/llama`.\n- Auth resolution order: `--api-key` flag → `~/.pi/agent/auth.json` → env vars →\n  `models.json` custom providers.\n- `/login` does OAuth for subscription providers.\n\n**⚠ The cost trap.** Per the docs: *\"Third-party harness usage draws from extra usage\nand is billed per token, not against Claude plan limits.\"* So running your Claude\nsubscription through Pi does **not** use your plan allowance — it bills per token on\ntop. That materially changes the economics of \"just swap the brain,\" and the video\nnever mentions it.\n\n## Context management — genuinely good\n\n- **Auto-compaction** fires when `contextTokens > contextWindow - reserveTokens`\n  (16,384 default). Finds a cut point working backward to `keepRecentTokens` (20,000\n  default), summarises everything older, keeps recent work intact.\n- **Manual:** `/compact [instructions]` with optional focus.\n- Tool results truncate to 2,000 chars during serialisation.\n- **`/tree`** navigates a branching session history. Switching branches offers a\n  `BranchSummaryEntry` that carries context from the abandoned branch into the new one.\n\n```json\n{ \"compaction\": { \"enabled\": true, \"reserveTokens\": 16384, \"keepRecentTokens\": 20000 } }\n```\n\n## Settings — two layers, recursive merge\n\n| Layer | Path |\n|---|---|\n| Global | `~/.pi/agent/settings.json` |\n| Project | `.pi/settings.json` |\n\nProject wins, and **nested objects merge recursively** rather than replacing wholesale.\nThat is a better version of the promotion rule from the video — project settings\noverride globals field by field instead of clobbering them.\n\nNotable keys: `defaultProvider` `defaultModel` `defaultThinkingLevel`\n`modelThinkingLevels` `skills` `packages` `extensions` `prompts` `themes`\n`enableSkillCommands` `compaction.*` `branchSummary.reserveTokens` `theme`\n`externalEditor` `quietStartup` `tuiMode` `transport` `httpProxy` `retry` `terminal.*`\n`images.*`.\n\n`enableSkillCommands` registers every skill as `/skill:name` — worth turning on.\n\n## Docs map\n\nGetting Started (Quickstart, Using Pi, Providers) · Core (llama.cpp, Security,\nContainerization, Settings, Keybindings, Sessions, Compaction) · Customization\n(Extensions, Skills, Prompt templates, Themes, Pi packages, Custom models, Custom\nproviders) · Integration (SDK, RPC mode, JSON event stream mode, TUI components) ·\nReference (Environment variables, Session format) · Platform Setup (Windows, Termux,\ntmux, Terminal setup, Shell aliases).\n\n**Four operating modes:** interactive TUI, print/JSON, RPC, and SDK. The JSON event\nstream and RPC modes are what make Pi scriptable from your existing KeystoneOS jobs.\n\n## My recommendation\n\n**Try it, in a sandbox, as a second harness — not as a replacement.**\n\nThe migration is far cheaper than I estimated this morning: skills are a one-line\nsetting (and `~/.agents/skills/` is already discovered), and `CLAUDE.md` loads as-is.\nWhat does *not* come free is the safety layer you have built — every permission prompt\nand guard is yours to re-create as an extension.\n\nSuggested first session, ~30 minutes:\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p ~/pi-trial && cd ~/pi-trial\nnpm install -g --ignore-scripts @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\npi\n# then: /login   (or export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY first)\n# then ask it to summarise a scratch repo\n```\n\nThen, if it feels good, port **one** hook — `dcg` is the obvious first, since the docs\nship a near-identical example — and see how long it actually takes. That one data point\ntells you whether the full port is a weekend or a month.\n\n## Open questions\n\n- Does `SYSTEM.md` exist? Homepage says yes, docs pages do not mention it.\n- What does Pi's default system prompt actually contain? Docs say \"minimal with\n  progressive capability disclosure\" — worth reading before trusting it with anything.\n- Do Claude Code **plugins** (your 6 enabled ones) have a Pi equivalent, or only\n  skills? \"Pi packages\" look like the analogue but I have not confirmed the mapping.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"772b8e64-fbb0-4e12-a595-9a42ffe1af3d","session_id":"f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"f1097c6d-9db5-4bb5-a0f7-c42d645cf75d","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main"}
